 a self-contained module hierarchy (`src/modules/*`).\n   4 - **Complete Firebase Client Removal**: All client-side Firebase SDK dependencies have been removed. Authentication is now strictly local (SQLite backend + Cookie management), and the vector store is decoupled from Firestore persistence.\n   5 - **Unified AI Flow Ecosystem**: 40+ Genkit flows have been categorized and standardized with a unified schema registry, ensuring 100% type safety and zero circular dependencies.\n   6 - **Performance Optimization Pass**: High-frequency components (Report Builder) now utilize atomic Zustand selectors and `React.memo` to minimize virtual DOM overhead.\n   7 - **DI for React**: Introduced a standardized `useService(token)` pattern for resolving dependencies within the React lifecycle.\n   8 - **Zone Tabs Architecture Implementation**: Successfully transitioned the main `ReportModeGenerator` to a professional 3-zone architecture (Top/Left/Center/Right).\n   9 - **Production Grade UI Overhaul**: Modernized all critical document production workflows, including Blueprinting (TOC), Construction (Build), and Production (Export) manifests.\n  10 - **Visual Identity Synchronization**: Unified the brand aesthetic across the Home Page, Global Header, and interactive Cover Page editor.\n  11 - **Writing Tools Modernization**: Completed a high-fidelity overhaul of flagship specialist tools (**Summarizer, Researcher, Stylist, Document Writer, Blueprint, Quiz, UI Description, Idea Generator, Lecture Generator, Session Plan, Assignments Solver**), transitioning them to the Manifest Workspace layout.\n  12 - **Platform-Wide Aesthetic Unification**: Standardized typography (`serif 15px`), layout grids, and interactive patterns across all core production surfaces.\n  13 - **Admin Console Modernization**: Transitioned the internal management suite to a professional 3-zone layout with technical HUD navigation and manifest-style management consoles for core system operations.\n  14 - **AI Module Resilience Enhancement**: Implemented tiered model fallback (Llama 3 \u2192 Phi-3 \u2192 Gemma 2B) and proactive request timeouts to ensure system stability in 8GB RAM environments.\n  15 - **Domain Entity Property Pattern Standardization**: Standardized all domain entities on the `toProperties()`, `toStateObject()`, and `withProps()` pattern for type-safe serialization and cloning.\n  16 - **Client-Server SDK Decoupling**: Successfully decoupled heavy AI SDK dependencies from the client bundle by relocating constants and implementing strict runtime guards.\n  17 \n````", "Successfully opened memory-bank/systemPatterns.md:\n\n````markdown\n   0 # System Patterns - Arkan\n   1 \n   2 ## Architectural Patterns\n   3 \n   4 ### 1. Strict Clean Architecture (DDD)\n   5 **Purpose**: Absolute separation of concerns between business logic, orchestration, and infrastructure.\n   6 - **Domain Layer**: Pure entities, repository interfaces, and service interfaces. Zero external dependencies (except for basic utils).\n   7 - **Application Layer**: Use Cases that orchestrate domain logic. Implementation-agnostic.\n   8 - **Adapter Layer**: Controllers (API routes), Hooks (logic-heavy), State (Zustand), and Smart Components.\n   9 - **Infrastructure Layer**: Concrete implementations (SQLite, Qdrant, SMTP, JWT).\n  10 \n  11 ### 2. Modular AI & Schema Pattern\n  12 **Purpose**: Standardized approach for AI operations with unified data contracts.\n  13 - **Implementation**: Categorized flows in `src/modules/ai/infrastructure/flows/`.\n  14 - **Unified Schemas**: Single source of truth for tool/flow Zod schemas in `infrastructure/tools/schemas.ts`.\n  15 - **Literal Enforcement**: Using `as const` for union-based output types.\n  16 \n  17 ### 3. Dependency Injection (tsyringe)\n  18 **Purpose**: Decoupling components and services for testability and inversion of control.\n  19 - **Implementation**: Token-based injection in `src/infrastructure/di/container.ts`.\n  20 - **React Integration**: Standardized `useService(token)` hook for client-side resolution.\n  21 \n  22 ### 4. Functional Injection Pattern\n  23 **Purpose**: Maintaining domain purity for complex transformations.\n  24 - **Implementation**: Domain methods (e.g., `Project.applyTemplate`) accept infrastructure functions as callbacks rather than importing them directly.\n  25 \n  26 ### 5. Standardized Domain Property Pattern\n  27 **Purpose**: Enforcing type safety and predictable state management for domain entities.\n  28 - **Implementation**: All domain entities follow a strict contract:\n  29     - `toProperties()`: Primary serialization method returning plain data objects.\n  30     - `toStateObject()`: Backward-compatible alias for serialization.\n  31     - `withProps()`: Standardized cloning method using deep copies for complex fields.\n  32 - **Benefits**: Eliminates naming conflicts, ensures deep immutability, and simplifies hydration.\n  33 \n  34 ### 6. Tiered Model Fallback Pattern\n  35 **Purpose**: Ensuring system availability in hardware-constrained (8GB RAM) environments.\n  36 - **Implementation**: `GenkitAiService.runFlow` attempts a tiered fallback (e.g., Llama 3 \u2192 Phi-3 \u2192 Gemma 2B) upon failure.\n  37 - **Benefits**: Prevents local server crashes and provides a graceful degradation of service quality.\n  38 \n  39 ## RAG & Vector Search Pattern\n  40 \n  41 ### **Purpose**\n  42 To provide relevant, up-to-date, and domain-specific context to AI models, reducing hallucinations and improving response accuracy.\n  43 \n  44 ### **Architecture**\n  45 1.  **Indexing Pipeline**:\n  46     - **Input**: Documents (PDF, DOCX, TXT, MD).\n  47     - **Process**:\n  48         1.  `File Extraction`: Text is extracted from documents.\n  49         2.  `Chunking`: Text is split into smaller, overlapping chunks.\n  50         3.  `Embedding`: Each chunk is converted into a vector using Ollama (`nomic-embed-text`).\n  51         4.  `Storage`: Vectors and metadata are stored in Qdrant.\n  52     - **Implementation**: `src/modules/rag/infrastructure/indexer.ts`\n  53 \n  54 2.  **Retrieval Pipeline**:\n  55     - **Input**: User query (string).\n  56     - **Process**:\n  57         1.  `Query Embedding`: The query is converted into a vector.\n  58         2.  `Vector Search`: Qdrant performs a similarity search (Cosine) to find the most relevant chunks.\n  59         3.  `Filtering`: Results are filtered by `projectId`, `userId`, etc.\n  60         4.  `Re-ranking`: Results are re-ordered using a hybrid approach (semantic + keyword) for better relevance.\n  61         5.  `Context Formatting`: Top results are formatted into a context string for the LLM.\n  62         6.  `Token Truncation`: Context is proactively truncated using `TokenManager` to fit within the specific model's context window.\n  63     - **Implementation**: `src/modules/rag/infrastructure/retrieval.ts`, `src/modules/rag/infrastructure/reranker.ts`, and `src/modules/ai/domain/utils/TokenManager.ts`\n  64 \n  65 ### **Components**\n  66 - **Vector Store**: `src/modules/rag/infrastructure/vector-store.ts` (Factory pattern to switch between in-memory and Qdrant).\n  67 - **Qdrant Adapter**: `src/modules/rag/infrastructure/qdrant-adapter.ts` (Production-ready implementation).\n  68 - **Embedding Service**: `src/modules/rag/infrastructure/embeddings.ts` (Handles communication with Ollama).\n  69 - **Caching**: `src/modules/rag/infrastructure/cache.ts` (In-memory LRU cache for embeddings and search results).\n  70 \n  71 ### **Benefits**\n  72 - **Reduces Hallucinations**: Grounds AI responses in factual data.\n  73 - **Enables Domain-Specific Knowledge**: Allows the AI to use proprietary or project-specific information.\n  74 - **Keeps Knowledge Current**: The knowledge base can be updated without re-training the model.\n  75 \n  76 ## Conversational AI Pattern (Project Consultant)\n  77 \n  78 ### **Purpose**\n  79 To provide users with a real-time, context-aware AI assistant that can answer questions, provide feedback, and offer suggestions about their currently active project.\n  80 \n  81 ### **Architecture**\n  82 1.  **Frontend Component (`ProjectChat.tsx`)**:\n  83     - A floating chat window that can be toggled by the user.\n  84     - Manages the display of the conversation history.\n  85     - Captures user input and triggers the AI interaction.\n  86 \n  87 2.  **Client-side Hook (`useProjectChat.ts`)**:\n  88     - Manages the state of the chat session (messages, loading status).\n  89     - Retrieves the full context of the current project from the `useBuilderStore`.\n  90     - Uses React Query's `useMutation` to call the backend API endpoint.\n  91     - Appends user messages and AI responses to the conversation history for a seamless chat experience.\n  92 \n  93 3.  **API Route (`/api/chat/project-consultant`)**:\n  94     - A secure endpoint protected by the `withAuth` middleware.\n  95     - Receives the user's message, the full project context, and the conversation history.\n  96     - Calls the corresponding Genkit AI flow (`projectConsultantFlow`).\n  97 \n  98 4.  **AI Flow (`projectConsultantFlow`)**:\n  99     - A Genkit flow specifically designed for conversational consultation.\n 100     - It receives the project context and conversation history and injects them into a system prompt.\n 101     - The system prompt instructs the AI to act as an expert consultant, using the provided project data to give relevant advice.\n 102     - It processes the user's immediate message to generate a helpful, context-aware response.\n 103 \n 104 ### **Benefits**\n 105 - **Context-Aware Assistance**: The AI has full knowledge of the user's current project, allowing it to provide highly relevant and specific advice.\n 106 - **Interactive Experience**: Transforms the static document-building process into a dynamic conversation.\n 107 - **Improved Productivity**: Users can get instant feedback and ideas without leaving their workspace.\n 108 \n 109 ## State Management Patterns\n 110 \n 111 ### 1. Zustand Store Pattern (Client-side)\n 112 **Purpose**: Lightweight global state management for UI and session data.\n 113 - **Implementation**: `src/modules/[domain]/adapters/state/`\n 114 - **Usage**: Manages the active project, application status (`loading`, `dirty`, `saving`), and user settings. Stores are co-located with their domain.\n 115 \n 116 ### 2. React Query Pattern (Server-side Cache)\n 117 **Purpose**: Server state management with caching, refetching, and synchronization.\n 118 - **Implementation**: Domain-specific hooks in `src/modules/[domain]/adapters/hooks/`.\n 119 - **Benefits**: Reduces redundant API calls, provides optimistic updates, handles background refetching. Uses centralized key factory in `src/infrastructure/api/query-keys.ts`.\n 120 \n 121 ## UI/UX & Architecture Patterns\n 122 \n 123 ### 1. 3-Zone Workspace Architecture (IDE Pattern)\n 124 **Purpose**: Maximizing focus and logical flow for complex document production.\n 125 - **Top Zone**: Horizontal bar for project identity and Session Actions (Export, Save).\n 126 - **Left Zone**: Primary navigation and Setup Tools (Files, Templates, Chapters).\n 127 - **Center Zone**: Primary work area (A4 preview, Editor) with a dedicated status HUD.\n 128 - **Right Zone**: Context-sensitive AI controls, behavioral rules, and chat.\n 129 \n 130 ### 2. Production Manifest Pattern\n 131 **Purpose**: Standardized, high-trust model for complex multi-step operations.\n 132 - **Implementation**: Used in Build Project, Export, Browse Library dialogs, and standalone Writing Tools.\n 133 - **Features**: Technical header with activity pulse, data-dense body, and versioned footer.\n 134 \n 135 ### 3. Manifest Workspace Architecture\n 136 **Purpose**: Specialized layout for standalone intelligent tools (Summarizer, Researcher, Stylist).\n 137 - **Setup Zone (Left)**: Technical side-panel (400-450px) for configuration protocols and logic definitions.\n 138 - **Results Canvas (Right)**: Immersive, high-fidelity work area with radial grid textures and shadow-inner effects.\n 139 - **Interactive HUDs**: Floating controls for view switching (e.g., Comparison vs. Logic Diff).\n 140 \n 141 ### 4. Sidebar Dashboard Density\n 142 **Purpose**: Maintaining professional aesthetics and readability in narrow sidebars (20-25%).\n 143 - **Styling**: `text-[10px]` bold uppercase tracking for labels, `bg-muted/5` for input backgrounds.\n 144 - **Hierarchy**: Priority-based tab reordering (e.g., Context -> Prompt -> Controls).\n 145 \n 146 ### 5. Contextual HUD Pattern\n 147 **Purpose**: Keeping secondary document utilities accessible without cluttering toolbars.\n 148 - **Implementation**: Floating, semi-transparent glass-morphism containers (e.g., Zoom HUD, Syntax Guides).\n 149 \n 150 ### 5. State Lifting for Zone Sync\n 151 **Purpose**: Seamless coordination between interactive UI zones.\n 152 - **Pattern**: Lifting active selection state (Chapter/Section) to the main page hook to automatically focus relevant sidebar panels.\n 153 \n 154 ## Error Handling Patterns\n 155 \n 156 ### 1. Centralized API Error Handling\n 157 **Purpose**: Consistent error handling for all API routes.\n 158 - **Implementation**: `src/infrastructure/api/api-utils.ts` (`handleApiError`)\n 159 - **Benefits**: Standardized error responses, utilizes the **Result Pattern** for explicit error flow throughout the stack.\n 160 \n 161 ### 2. Document Processing Error Handling\n 162 **Purpose**: Robust error handling for document generation (DOCX, PPTX).\n 163 - **Implementation**: `src/lib/word-converter/ast-converter.ts`, `src/lib/utils/ProxiedImage.ts`\n 164 - **Features**: Graceful fallbacks for invalid images and malformed tables, preventing export crashes.\n 165 \n 166 ## Security Patterns\n 167 \n 168 ### 1. Authentication Guard HOF\n 169 **Purpose**: Protecting API routes and server actions from unauthorized access.\n 170 - **Implementation**: `src/lib/utils/api-utils.ts` (`withAuth`)\n 171 - **Benefits**: Centralized auth logic, support for admin-only routes.\n 172 \n 173 ### 2. Image Proxy\n 174 **Purpose**: Avoids CORS issues and provides a security layer for fetching external images.\n 175 - **Implementation**: `src/app/api/proxy/convert/route.ts`\n 176 - **Features**: Whitelists allowed domains to prevent abuse (SSRF).\n 177 \n 178 ### 3. Local File Storage\n 179 **Purpose**: Self-contained file management without cloud dependencies.\n 180 - **Service**: `LocalFileStorageService` (implements `IFileStorageService`).\n 181 - **Storage**: Files are persisted in the root `storage/uploads/` directory.\n 182 - **Serving**: Files are served through a secure API route `src/app/api/storage/[...path]/route.ts` with path traversal protection.\n 183 \n 184 ## Developer Experience Patterns\n 185 \n 186 ### 1. Interactive API Documentation\n 187 **Purpose**: Provide developers with a clear, interactive way to understand and test the application's API.\n 188 - **Implementation**:\n 189   - **Configuration**: `src/config/api-docs.ts` - A single source of truth defining all API endpoints, their methods, descriptions, authentication levels, and example request/response bodies.\n 190   - **UI Components**: Reusable components in `src/components/api-docs/` such as `EndpointCard`, `ApiTester`, and `CodeBlock` create a dynamic and maintainable documentation page.\n 191   - **Page**: `/app/api-docs/page.tsx` - The main page that renders the documentation from the configuration file.\n 192 - **Benefits**:\n 193   - **Self-Documenting**: The API documentation is tightly coupled with its definition, reducing the chance of it becoming outdated.\n 194   - **Interactive Testing**: Developers can make live API calls directly from the documentation UI, speeding up testing and integration.\n 195   - **Clarity**: Provides a clear contract for each endpoint, including required parameters and expected response structures.\n 196   - **Maintainability**: Adding or updating API documentation is as simple as updating the central configuration file.\n 197 \n````"], "tool_call_id": "pyd_ai_f68fd45beafe4e40b9604d109fb1e9d5", "metadata": null, "timestamp": "2026-04-05T05:42:08.557977+00:00", "part_kind": "tool-return"}
